{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15568 - PROTEIN:6147",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate may interact with Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9. This interaction is mediated through a series of targets, all of which are members of the SDR family, suggesting a common pathway involving enzymatic activity. The evidence points to Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate acting as a substrate for a sialyltransferase, which in turn is targeted by multiple compounds, including (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide. These compounds are known to inhibit the SDR family, implying that Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate may indirectly influence the activity of this family through its role as a substrate for the sialyltransferase. The presence of multiple independent pathways converging on the SDR family member 9 suggests a robust, mechanistically supported link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The evidence supports a plausible, biologically relevant mechanism, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct relationship between Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate and the SDR family member 9.",
